[LINK: (9, Premcourt) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Churchgate]   [LINK: (Churchgate) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/South_Mumbai] ,  [LINK: (Mumbai) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mumbai_City_district]

Ajit Anantrao Pawar [LINK: ([a]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ajit_Pawar#cite_note-2]  (22 July 1959 – 28 January 2026) was an Indian politician who served as  [LINK: (Maharashtra's) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Maharashtra]  longest-serving  [LINK: (deputy chief minister) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_deputy_chief_ministers_of_Maharashtra]  until his  [LINK: (death) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2026_Baramati_Learjet_45_crash]  in 2026. [LINK: ([2]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ajit_Pawar#cite_note-3]  [LINK: ([3]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ajit_Pawar#cite_note-4]  He held the office for six terms under various governments including the cabinets of  [LINK: (Prithviraj Chavan) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Prithviraj_Chavan_ministry] ,  [LINK: (Devendra Fadnavis) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chief_ministership_of_Devendra_Fadnavis] ,  [LINK: (Uddhav Thackeray) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Thackeray_ministry] , and  [LINK: (Eknath Shinde) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Eknath_Shinde_ministry] . [LINK: ([4]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ajit_Pawar#cite_note-5]

He also served as the  [LINK: (leader of the opposition) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_leaders_of_the_opposition_in_the_Maharashtra_Legislative_Assembly]  in the  [LINK: (Maharashtra Legislative Assembly) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Maharashtra_Legislative_Assembly]  from 2022 to 2023 and represented  [LINK: (Baramati Lok Sabha constituency) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Baramati_Lok_Sabha_constituency]  in 1991. [LINK: ([5]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ajit_Pawar#cite_note-6]


# ANCESTRY #

Pawar's grandparents were Govindrao and Sharda Pawar. [LINK: ([6]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ajit_Pawar#cite_note-:1-7]  [LINK: ([7]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ajit_Pawar#cite_note-:3-8]  Govindrao was associated with  [LINK: (worker cooperatives) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Worker_cooperative]  in  [LINK: (Baramati) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Baramati] , while Sharda managed the family farm. [LINK: ([6]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ajit_Pawar#cite_note-:1-7]  In 1938, she was elected to the Pune local board. [LINK: ([7]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ajit_Pawar#cite_note-:3-8]  The couple had eleven children, including seven sons and four daughters; [LINK: ([7]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ajit_Pawar#cite_note-:3-8]  one of their sons, named  [LINK: (Sharad Pawar) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sharad_Pawar] , later became the president of the  [LINK: (Nationalist Congress Party) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nationalist_Congress_Party]  (NCP) and a four-time  [LINK: (chief minister of Maharashtra) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chief_Minister_of_Maharashtra] . [LINK: ([6]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ajit_Pawar#cite_note-:1-7]


# EARLY LIFE #

Ajit Anantrao Pawar [LINK: ([8]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ajit_Pawar#cite_note-9]  was born on 22 July 1959 in  [LINK: (Deolali Pravara) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Deolali_Pravara] ,  [LINK: (Maharashtra) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Maharashtra] , [LINK: ([9]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ajit_Pawar#cite_note-:2-10]  [LINK: ([10]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ajit_Pawar#cite_note-11]  to Anantrao and Ashatai Pawar. [LINK: ([7]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ajit_Pawar#cite_note-:3-8]  His father died when he was eighteen years old. [LINK: ([9]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ajit_Pawar#cite_note-:2-10]  He had a brother named Shriniwas and a sister named Vijaya. [LINK: ([6]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ajit_Pawar#cite_note-:1-7]  In 1985, [LINK: ([11]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ajit_Pawar#cite_note-12]  he married  [LINK: (Sunetra Pawar) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sunetra_Pawar]  (née Patil), the daughter of  [LINK: (Padamsinh Bajirao Patil) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Padamsinh_Bajirao_Patil] , a former minister in Maharashtra. [LINK: ([6]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ajit_Pawar#cite_note-:1-7]  The couple had two sons, Jay and Parth Pawar. [LINK: ([12]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ajit_Pawar#cite_note-13]

Following the footsteps of his uncle  [LINK: (Sharad Pawar) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sharad_Pawar]  in the  [LINK: (Indian National Congress) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Indian_National_Congress] , [LINK: ([13]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ajit_Pawar#cite_note-14]  Ajit Pawar made his first foray into politics in 1982 when he was elected to the board of a  [LINK: (cooperative sugar factory) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cooperative_sugar_factories_in_Maharashtra] . In 1991, he was elected as the chairman of the  [LINK: (Pune District Central Cooperative Bank) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pune_District_Central_Cooperative_Bank]  and remained in the post for the next 16 years. [LINK: ([14]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ajit_Pawar#cite_note-15]


# POLITICAL CAREER #

Pawar was elected to the  [LINK: (Lok Sabha) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lok_Sabha]  for the first time in 1991 from the  [LINK: (Baramati Parliamentary constituency) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Baramati_Lok_Sabha_constituency] . He later vacated the seat for his uncle, who then became the  [LINK: (Defence Minister) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Minister_of_Defence_(India)]  in Prime Minister  [LINK: (P. V. Narasimha Rao) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/P._V._Narasimha_Rao] 's government. [LINK: ([15]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ajit_Pawar#cite_note-16]  Pawar was known for his blunt and pragmatic leadership style, nicknamed "Ajit Dada". [LINK: ([16]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ajit_Pawar#cite_note-17]  [LINK: ([17]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ajit_Pawar#cite_note-18]

Later, he was elected to the  [LINK: (Maharashtra Legislative Assembly) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Maharashtra_Legislative_Assembly]  seven times from the  [LINK: (Baramati Assembly constituency) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Baramati_Assembly_constituency] . He first won in a 1991  [LINK: (by-election) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/By-election]  and subsequently retained it for five consecutive terms in 1995, 1999, 2004, 2009, and 2014. [LINK: ([18]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ajit_Pawar#cite_note-19]  [LINK: ([19]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ajit_Pawar#cite_note-20]  [LINK: ([20]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ajit_Pawar#cite_note-21]  He served as the Minister of State for Agriculture and Power in CM  [LINK: (Sudhakarrao Naik) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sudhakarrao_Naik] 's government from 1991 to 1992. [LINK: ([21]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ajit_Pawar#cite_note-auto-22]

He became the Minister of State for Soil Conservation, Power and Planning in 1992 when Sharad Pawar became the Chief Minister. In  [LINK: (1999) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/1999_Maharashtra_Legislative_Assembly_election] , as part of the INC-NCP coalition government, he became a Cabinet Minister responsible for the Irrigation Department. He was additionally given the Rural Development Department in 2003 as part of  [LINK: (Sushilkumar Shinde) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sushilkumar_Shinde] 's cabinet. [LINK: ([21]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ajit_Pawar#cite_note-auto-22]  After the INC-NCP coalition won in the  [LINK: (2004 Assembly elections) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2014_Maharashtra_Legislative_Assembly_election] , he retained the Water Resources Ministry in Deshmukh's and later  [LINK: (Ashok Chavan) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ashok_Chavan] 's cabinets. [LINK: ([22]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ajit_Pawar#cite_note-23]

There were allegations that, as the minister for water resources, he helped the development of  [LINK: (Lavasa) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lavasa] , [LINK: ([23]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ajit_Pawar#cite_note-24]  a project touted as a "vision of Sharad Pawar". The Maharashtra Krishna Valley Development Corporation (MKVDC) leased 141.15 ha (348.8 acres) to Lavasa in August 2002, which included part of the Warasgaon dam reservoir. The lease between MKVDC and Lavasa was executed at rates far below the market rate. [LINK: ([24]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ajit_Pawar#cite_note-25]

In September 2012, there were accusations that there had been misappropriation to the tune of Rs. 70,000 crores. These allegations were made by the Maharashtra bureaucrat  [LINK: (Vijay Pandhare) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Vijay_Pandhare] , and caused the anti-corruption activist  [LINK: (Anjali Damania) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Anjali_Damania]  to demand Pawar's resignation as a minister. However, the allegations were not proved, and Ajit was reinstated as the Deputy CM of Maharashtra. [LINK: ([25]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ajit_Pawar#cite_note-26]

On 7 April 2013, Pawar's statement at a speech in  [LINK: (Indapur) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Indapur]  sparked controversy due to its alleged callousness. In response to a 55-day fast by activists protesting the Maharashtra government's inability to provide water during a drought, he asked whether he should "urinate into [the dam]" to make up for the lack of water in it. After a public outcry against his statement, he publicly apologised, saying that the comment was the "biggest mistake of [his] life". [LINK: ([26]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ajit_Pawar#cite_note-27]

According to reports, Ajit Pawar made a phone call to the Pune Police Commissioner on 19 May 2024, after Vedant Agarwal (son of Vishal Agarwal, owner of Brahma Realty & Infrastructure), then 17 and allegedly drunk,  [LINK: (crashed a luxury Porsche) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2024_Pune_Porsche_car_crash]  in Pune's  [LINK: (Kalyani Nagar) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kalyani_Nagar] , killing two IT professionals on a motorbike. Pawar allegedly urged the police not to harshly pursue the case against the minor accused. This was widely described as political pressure. [LINK: ([27]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ajit_Pawar#cite_note-auto1-28]  Pawar did not deny calling the police commissioner, but said it was to ensure the police did not face other pressures, portraying it as routine oversight by a guardian minister rather than interference. Pawar also described the allegations as baseless. [LINK: ([27]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ajit_Pawar#cite_note-auto1-28]


# LEADER OF NATIONALIST CONGRESS PARTY #

On 23 November 2019, he defected from NCP and joined a government led by the  [LINK: (Bharatiya Janata Party) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bharatiya_Janata_Party]  and became the  [LINK: (Deputy Chief Minister) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Deputy_Chief_Minister_of_Maharashtra] . [LINK: ([28]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ajit_Pawar#cite_note-29]  [LINK: ([29]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ajit_Pawar#cite_note-30]  He submitted a paper with the signatures of NCP's MLAs to the Governor to prove the government's majority. However, the government collapsed less than 80 hours later and he resigned alongside then-CM  [LINK: (Devendra Fadnavis) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Devendra_Fadnavis] . He subsequently returned to the NCP, and on 1 December 2019, it was announced that he would take over as Deputy CM for the  [LINK: (Maha Vikas Aghadi) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Maha_Vikas_Aghadi]  government after the start of the winter session of the state legislature on 16 December. [LINK: ([30]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ajit_Pawar#cite_note-31]

In 2022, due to  [LINK: (a split in the Shiv Sena) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2022_Maharashtra_political_crisis#Initial_Breakout] , the  [LINK: (Maha Vikas Aghadi) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Maha_Vikas_Aghadi]  government collapsed. After the rebel Shiv Sena faction and BJP formed a government with  [LINK: (Eknath Shinde) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Eknath_Shinde]  as CM, Pawar became the Leader of the Opposition in the  [LINK: (Maharashtra Legislative Assembly) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Maharashtra_Legislative_Assembly] . [LINK: ([31]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ajit_Pawar#cite_note-32]

In 2023, having the support of the majority of the erstwhile NCP's MLAs, he also claimed the position of president of the NCP, as well as the party's name and its  [LINK: (electoral symbol) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Electoral_symbol] . [LINK: ([32]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ajit_Pawar#cite_note-33]  [LINK: ([33]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ajit_Pawar#cite_note-:0-34]  Pawar joined the ruling  [LINK: (Maha Yuti) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Maha_Yuti]  coalition and took the oath as deputy CM of the state on 2 July. On 7 February 2024, the  [LINK: (Election Commission Of India (ECI)) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Election_Commission_of_India]  awarded the party name and symbol to the  [LINK: (faction) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nationalist_Congress_Party_(Ajit_Pawar)]  headed by Ajit. The faction led by Sharad Pawar will be henceforth known as  [LINK: (Nationalist Congress Party (Sharadchandra Pawar)) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nationalist_Congress_Party_(Sharadchandra_Pawar)] . [LINK: ([34]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ajit_Pawar#cite_note-35]  [LINK: ([33]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ajit_Pawar#cite_note-:0-34]  Despite his faction being routed in the  [LINK: (2024 Lok Sabha election) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2024_Indian_general_election_in_Maharashtra] , his NCP placed in third in the  [LINK: (2024 Legislative Assembly election) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2024_Maharashtra_Legislative_Assembly_election] , with the Maha Yuti alliance forming a landslide majority government; Pawar was again sworn-in as deputy chief minister in December, tasked with portfolios such as  [LINK: (finance) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ministry_of_Finance_(Maharashtra)] . The party also performed well in the  [LINK: (2025–26 Maharashtra local elections) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%9326_Maharashtra_local_elections] . Months before his death, reconciliation talks between the two NCP parties were reportedly occurring. [LINK: ([35]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ajit_Pawar#cite_note-36]  [LINK: ([36]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ajit_Pawar#cite_note-37]  His wife,  [LINK: (Rajya Sabha) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rajya_Sabha]  MP  [LINK: (Sunetra) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sunetra_Pawar] , would succeed him as party president and deputy chief minister.


# DEATH #

On 28 January 2026, at approximately 08:44  [LINK: (IST) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Indian_Standard_Time]  ( [LINK: (UTC+5:30) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/UTC%2B5:30] ), a business jet carrying Pawar on an air charter flight from  [LINK: (Chhatrapati Shivaji Maharaj International Airport) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chhatrapati_Shivaji_Maharaj_International_Airport]  in  [LINK: (Mumbai) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mumbai]  to  [LINK: (Baramati Airport) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Baramati_Airport]  in  [LINK: (Maharashtra) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Maharashtra]  crashed while attempting a second approach to Baramati Airport Runway 11. The aircraft veered off the runway, burst into flames, and was destroyed on impact, resulting in the deaths of Pawar and four others on board. [LINK: ([37]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ajit_Pawar#cite_note-38]  [LINK: ([38]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ajit_Pawar#cite_note-39]  [LINK: ([39]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ajit_Pawar#cite_note-40]  [LINK: ([40]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ajit_Pawar#cite_note-41]  [LINK: ([41]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ajit_Pawar#cite_note-42]

The aircraft was a 16-year-old  [LINK: (Learjet 45XR) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Learjet_45] , registration VT-SSK, operated by VSR Aviation. [LINK: ([42]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ajit_Pawar#cite_note-43]  [LINK: ([43]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ajit_Pawar#cite_note-44]

At the time of the incident, Pawar was travelling to  [LINK: (Baramati) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Baramati]  to address multiple public meetings in connection with the upcoming  [LINK: (Zilla Parishad) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/District_council_(India)]  elections. [LINK: ([44]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ajit_Pawar#cite_note-45]  The Maharashtra government declared a three-day state of mourning following his death; his funeral was held in Baramati on 29 January, with full state honours. His body was  [LINK: (cremated) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cremated] . [LINK: ([45]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ajit_Pawar#cite_note-46]  [LINK: ([46]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ajit_Pawar#cite_note-47]


